With every symbol error resolved, the content_shell link still fails -- but now
purely on memory:

    collect2: fatal error: ld terminated with signal 21 [Kill Thread]

ld had written 141,614,983 bytes of the ~190 MB binary when the kernel killed
it. This is a clean out-of-memory kill, not the VMArea::Unwire panic that the
kernel patch (haiku_kernel_patches/K0001) fixed; that panic no longer happens
and the link now runs to the end of its memory budget instead of taking the
machine down.

RCHROMIUM_LINK_LOWMEM=1 was already in effect, so --no-keep-memory and
--reduce-memory-overheads were on. The remaining levers were the two flags
AGENTS.md flagged as costly for exactly this reason: both make ld build a
whole-program view before it can write anything -- --gc-sections a
reachability graph over every section, -O2 a string tail-merge pass.

Freeing memory elsewhere was tried first and did not help: another session had
an app running on this machine, and stopping it recovered 1.4 MB. The shortfall
is ld's own working set, not competition for the box.

The cost is a larger binary. -ffunction-sections and -fdata-sections stay on
above, so the code is still split into per-symbol sections; nothing collects
the unused ones any more. That is an acceptable trade for a bootstrap: a bigger
binary that exists beats a smaller one that cannot be linked. Revisit if a
linker with a lower peak (lld) ever becomes available for this target -- neither
gold nor lld is present in this toolchain today.

Changing ldflags does not invalidate any object, so this costs one relink, not
a rebuild.

--- a/chromium/build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn
+++ b/chromium/build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn
@@ -2033,13 +2033,26 @@
       "-ffunction-sections",
     ]
 
-    common_optimize_on_ldflags += [
-      # Specifically tell the linker to perform optimizations.
-      # See http://lwn.net/Articles/192624/ .
-      # -O2 enables string tail merge optimization in gold and lld.
-      "-Wl,-O2",
-      "-Wl,--gc-sections",
-    ]
+    if (is_haiku) {
+      # Both of these make ld build a whole-program view before it can write
+      # anything: --gc-sections a reachability graph over every section, -O2 a
+      # string tail-merge pass. On the 2 GB VAIO that is what pushes the
+      # ~190 MB content_shell link past what the kernel will give it -- ld got
+      # as far as 141 MB and was killed ("ld terminated with signal 21
+      # [Kill Thread]") with --no-keep-memory and --reduce-memory-overheads
+      # already in effect. The cost is a larger binary, since the
+      # -ffunction-sections/-fdata-sections above still split everything out
+      # but nothing collects the unused parts afterwards. A bigger binary that
+      # exists beats a smaller one that cannot be linked.
+    } else {
+      common_optimize_on_ldflags += [
+        # Specifically tell the linker to perform optimizations.
+        # See http://lwn.net/Articles/192624/ .
+        # -O2 enables string tail merge optimization in gold and lld.
+        "-Wl,-O2",
+        "-Wl,--gc-sections",
+      ]
+    }
   }
 }
 
